**Yes — and the good news is you've already built ~80% of it.** `AuditLogService.log()` already does the realtime part: it persists the entry and immediately pushes it over SSE (`broadcast(...)` / `sendToUser(...)`). What's missing is the wiring to make it actually *work* — and there are two real bugs in the current path.

## What's broken right now

**1. Event-name mismatch (the reason you see nothing today)**

Your browser only listens for one event name:

```js
// index.html:752
eventSource.addEventListener("NOTIFICATION", (e) => { ... });
```

But the server emits audit events named after the action:

```java
// AuditLogService.java:47-49
notificationService.broadcast(action.name(), saved);      // "LISTING_CREATED", "USER_REGISTERED"...
notificationService.sendToUser(notifyUserId, action.name(), saved);
```

So every audit log *is* being pushed to the browser in real time — and the browser silently ignores all of it. Also, nothing in your backend ever emits `"NOTIFICATION"`, so even the intended toast path is dead.

**2. Privacy leak: `broadcast` goes to *everyone***

```java
if (notifyUserId == null) {
    notificationService.broadcast(action.name(), saved); // → every connected user
}
```

When no explicit recipient is passed (which is most call sites in `UserService`, `ListingService`, `AdminService`, `TransactionServices`), the full audit entry — actor username, details, admin actions — is sent to **all logged-in users**, not just admins. Fine for a demo, not fine otherwise.

**3. SSE is fire-and-forget** — if a user is offline or refreshes, the event is gone; `addToHistory()` only lives in the session.

## How to make it a proper realtime feed

### Step 1 — unify the event envelope
Emit one event name with a structured payload:

```java
// AuditLogService.java
AuditLog saved = auditRepository.save(entry);

Map<String, Object> payload = Map.of(
    "type",    action.name(),
    "title",   humanize(action),          // "Listing created"
    "message", details,
    "auditId", saved.getId(),
    "at",      saved.getCreatedAt().toString()
);

if (notifyUserId == null) {
    notificationService.sendToRole(User.Role.ADMIN, "NOTIFICATION", payload); // was: broadcast → everyone
} else {
    notificationService.sendToUser(notifyUserId, "NOTIFICATION", payload);
}
```

Now your existing front-end listener works unchanged, and toasts/badge/history light up immediately.

### Step 2 — add role-based routing to `NotificationService`
`emittersByUser` already exists; add a role bucket in `subscribe()`:

```java
private final Map<String, List<SseEmitter>> emittersByRole = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

public SseEmitter subscribe(User user) {          // change signature from Long userId
    SseEmitter emitter = new SseEmitter(Long.MAX_VALUE);
    emittersByUser.computeIfAbsent(user.getId(), k -> new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>()).add(emitter);
    emittersByRole.computeIfAbsent(user.getRole().name(), k -> new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>()).add(emitter);
    // ... existing cleanup, but also remove from the role bucket
    return emitter;
}

public void sendToRole(User.Role role, String eventName, Object data) { /* same loop as sendToUser */ }
```

`NotificationController.stream()` already has the `User` object — just pass it instead of `me.getId()`.

### Step 3 — don't lose events (pick one)
- **Simplest:** set the SSE event `id` to `saved.getId()` (`SseEmitter.event().id(...)...`). EventSource automatically sends `Last-Event-ID` on reconnect — store the last id per emitter and replay matching `audit_logs` rows from the DB on resubscribe.
- **Robust (my earlier suggestion #1):** add the `notifications` table, write a row alongside the audit entry, and on stream connect send everything unread, then mark read. Gives you an unread badge that survives restarts, plus a paginated history endpoint.

### Step 4 — harden the push path
- Push **after** the DB commit: if you ever wrap these calls in `@Transactional`, move the SSE send to `TransactionSynchronization.afterCommit`, otherwise clients can receive events for rows that later roll back.
- Consider making it non-blocking (`@Async` or an `ApplicationEventPublisher` listener) — right now one slow SSE client adds latency to whatever request triggered the log.
- Throttle noisy actions (e.g. `LISTING_VIEWED`-style events if you add view logging) — batch or rate-limit per user so the toast UI isn't flooded.

## Bonus: live admin activity dashboard
Since admins get the `NOTIFICATION` stream anyway, you can build a "Live Activity" panel on your admin page that just does:

```js
eventSource.addEventListener("NOTIFICATION", (e) => {
    const d = JSON.parse(e.data);
    if (d.type?.startsWith("LISTING_") || d.type?.startsWith("USER_")) prependRow(d); // prepend to the audit table
});
```

…and backfill the table on page load from your existing `getUserActivity` / admin audit endpoint — live events then simply prepend newest-first.

---

**Summary:** yes, your user/audit logs can drive realtime features, and the persistence + push plumbing already exists — you need to fix the event-name mismatch, route broadcasts to admins only, and add replay/backfill so events survive disconnects.

