payment.pool.queue-capacity=50
payment.pool.shutdown-wait-seconds=30
```

---

## Why each choice

| Decision | Reason |
|---|---|
| **Raw `ThreadPoolExecutor`, not Spring's `@Async`** | You asked to *see* thread pooling — this class is the pool itself, visible and configurable. `@Async` hides it behind magic and gives you no bounded queue or rejection control. |
| **`core == max`, no idle timeout** | A *fixed* pool is the concept you're being graded on. Elastic pools grow under load — the exact opposite of "bound the number of simultaneous payment locks." |
| **`AbortPolicy` (not `CallerRunsPolicy`)** | `CallerRunsPolicy` would run the payment **on the buyer's HTTP thread** when the queue is full — the request would block for the whole DB transaction, defeating the async design. Failing fast with 429 is correct here. |
| **Non-daemon threads** | Daemon threads get killed mid-transaction on JVM exit. Non-daemon + `@PreDestroy` wait = no payment ever dies halfway through committing. |
| **Sizing: 4 ≤ 10** | HikariCP default pool is 10 connections. 4 payment workers leave 6 connections for reads, SSE, admin, search. Configurable — you can demo different sizes. |
| **`RejectedExecutionException` surfaces to Step 4** | The orchestrator will catch it and translate to HTTP 429 "marketplace busy" — the honest, demo-worthy behavior. |

**One caveat I want you to be aware of:** `@PreDestroy` calls `paymentExecutor()` directly — in this config class that returns the same singleton bean, so it works, but don't call that method from *other* classes (inject the bean instead). I'll inject it by name (`@Qualifier("paymentExecutor")`) in Step 4.

