I went through every page. Here's what I found and exactly what I'd change.

## 1. Design audit — what's actually inconsistent

| Issue | Where | Impact |
|---|---|---|
| **Two different visual languages** | `index.html`/`style.css` use green `#2ecc71`; `profile.html`/`listing-detail.html` use a purple gradient `#6ec3f4 → #a06cd5` | Pages look like different sites |
| **Only the home page has a navbar** | profile, listing-detail, create-listing, login have no nav at all | You can't get back to browsing without editing the URL |
| **`style.css` is half-dead** | Every page also carries a large inline `<style>` block that re-defines `.stats-row`, `.search-btn`, `.section-title` differently | Conflicts; changes in one place silently do nothing |
| **Product images never display** | all 3 renderers only read base64 `img`; nothing reads `imgUrl` | The 9 seeded images you asked for are invisible (blank placeholders) |
| **Profile shows false data** | `profile.html` reads `data.email` and `data.bio`, but `/api/profile` returns neither (field is `profileBio`) | Email shows "N/A", bio is always empty |
| **Raw errors leak everywhere** | `alert("Error: " + err.message)` in listing-detail/create-listing/profile | Users see `HTTP error! status: 500` or raw exception text |

**My proposal for "same design":** one shared navbar on every page, keep the green logo as the brand, use the purple gradient for primary buttons (the newer pages already do), fix image rendering to prefer `imgUrl`, and move the repeated CSS into `style.css` instead of duplicating it per page. No redesign — just consistency.

## 2. Wallet on the profile page (needs a small backend)

| File | Content |
|---|---|
| `Model/Requests/MoneyRequest.java` *(new)* | `{ "amount": 50.00 }` |
| `Model/Responses/WalletStatus.java` *(new)* | `{ balance, updatedAt }` |
| `Service/WalletService.java` *(new)* | `getWallet`, `deposit`, `withdraw` |
| `Controller/WalletController.java` *(new)* | `GET /api/wallet`, `POST /api/wallet/deposit`, `POST /api/wallet/withdraw` |

Thread-safety comes free from what you already built — deposit/withdraw reuse the **atomic** `credit`/`debit` SQL:

```java
@Transactional
public WalletStatus withdraw(BigDecimal amount) {
    validate(amount);                                   // > 0, <= 100000
    Long userId = currentUserService.getCurrentUser().getId();
    ensureWallet(currentUserService.getCurrentUser());
    if (walletRepository.debit(userId, amount) == 0)    // check + debit in one UPDATE
        throw new InformationExistException("Not enough money in your wallet");
    return getWallet(userId);
}
```

UI: a **Wallet card** in the profile layout (same `.section-card` style) with the balance, an amount field, quick buttons (+10 / +50 / +100), **Add money** and **Withdraw**, then a live re-render of the balance. Instant top-up — no real payment gateway.

## 3. Buy Now → payment page

- `listing-detail.html`: Buy Now stops calling the legacy `/api/listings/{id}/buy` and navigates to `/payment/{id}`.
- New `templates/payment.html` + route `GET /payment/{listingId}` (added to `HomeController` and to the permit-list in `SecurityConfiguration`).
- Page contents (same card styling as listing-detail):
  - **Order summary**: image, title, seller, price
  - **Your balance** (from `GET /api/wallet`)
  - **Choose payment method** (radio cards)
  - **Pay** button → `POST /api/payments/{listingId}` → poll `GET /api/payments/{id}` every 400 ms → success screen or a friendly failure message.

The backend only charges wallets today. So the payment-method question is a real design choice — options below.

## 4. Friendly error messages (no raw exception text)

One shared file, `static/js/app-errors.js`, used by every page:

```js
const AppErrors = {
  http(status) {
    switch (status) {
      case 400: return 'That request could not be completed. Please check your details.';
      case 401: return 'Your session has expired. Please log in again.';
      case 403: return "You don't have permission to do that.";
      case 404: return 'We could not find what you were looking for.';
      case 429: return 'Too many attempts. Please wait a minute and try again.';
      case 500: return 'Something went wrong on our side. Nothing was charged.';
      default:  return 'Something went wrong. Please try again.';
    }
  },
  payment(reason) {
    return {
      ALREADY_SOLD:       'Sorry — someone else bought this item first.',
      INSUFFICIENT_FUNDS: 'Your wallet balance is too low. Add money and try again.',
      WALLET_MISSING:     'Your wallet is not set up yet. Please refresh and retry.',
      PAYMENT_NOT_FOUND:  'We could not find that payment.',
      QUEUE_FULL:         'We are very busy right now. Please try again in a moment.',
      SYSTEM_ERROR:       'The payment could not be completed. No money was taken.'
    }[reason] || 'The payment could not be completed. No money was taken.';
  }
};
```

Plus a backend catch-all so unexpected exceptions never reach the browser as a stack trace:

```java
@ExceptionHandler(Exception.class)
public ResponseEntity<Map<String,String>> handleUnexpected(Exception ex) {
    log.error("Unhandled error", ex);                    // details stay in the server log
    return ResponseEntity.status(500)
            .body(Map.of("message", "Something went wrong. Please try again."));
}
```

And the misleading `"Invalid login details"` on every validation error becomes the actual field message.

## Implementation order

| Stage | Work |
|---|---|
| **A** | Wallet API + profile response fix (email/bio/wallet balance) + catch-all error handler + `/payment` route/security |
| **B** | Shared navbar, `app-errors.js`, wallet card in profile, `imgUrl` rendering everywhere |
| **C** | `payment.html` + Buy Now redirect + polling/success/failure screens |
| **D** | Replace remaining raw errors on login/create-listing/index with friendly ones |

